'''IAA COMMISSION: FIRST REFERRALS — Justice Aoyama Announces Three Cases; Harujima, Kizan, Akamatsu Group; "The Teeth Are Biting"; Akamatsu Group Case Is "Exactly What the IAA Was Designed to Address"'''
IAA Commission Chair Aoyama Reiko announced the Commission's first three referrals: (1) Harujima — institutional abuse in the idol industry (ongoing); (2) Kizan — AI safety compliance audit (ongoing); (3) Akamatsu Group — corporate fraud and institutional abuse of employee pension funds (new). Aoyama: "The Commission has teeth. The teeth are biting. The Akamatsu Group case is exactly the kind of institutional abuse the IAA was designed to address — a powerful institution that exploited the people it was supposed to protect." The Okami Times notes: the IAA Commission's first three referrals cover the idol industry, AI safety, and corporate fraud. The scope is broad. The teeth are biting. The question is: will the Commission have the resources to investigate all three? And will the government fund the Commission adequately?

'''AKAMATSU GROUP: POLITICAL DONATIONS — ¥48M to LDP Over 5 Years; POIA Investigation; Three Donations Within 30 Days of SESC Audit Deadlines; "The Donations Were Legal. The Timing Was Not."'''
The Akamatsu Group's political donations are under review. SESC records show ¥48M donated to the LDP over five years (2021-2026). The donations were legal — within the limits of the Political Funds Control Act. But three donations totalling ¥12M were made within 30 days of SESC audit deadlines. POIA investigation: "The donations were legal. The timing was not. We are examining whether the donations were intended to influence audit outcomes." LDP spokesperson: "The LDP accepts legal donations from legal entities." The Okami Times notes: the donations were legal. The timing was not. The question is: did the donations influence the audits? Did the SESC's routine audit fail to detect ¥18B in missing pension funds because the Akamatsu Group donated ¥12M within 30 days of the audit deadline? The POIA must investigate. The connection between political donations and regulatory failure must be examined. The workers paid. The executives donated. The regulators did not audit. The connection must be investigated.

