'''COLUMN: TWO SYSTEMS, TWO WHISTLEBLOWERS — by Wakita Hiroko, Editor-in-Chief'''

Two systems failed this week. Two whistleblowers did not.

The Akamatsu Group embezzled ¥18B from employee pension funds over three years. The SESC — the Securities and Exchange Surveillance Commission — received quarterly reports. The reports were falsified. The SESC did not detect the falsification. The routine audit did not detect the discrepancy. A mid-level accountant detected the discrepancy. A whistleblower — not the system — exposed the fraud.

The Ministry of Environment approved 14 waste export licenses without inspection. Tanaka Hiroshi signed the licenses. He was told the inspections were completed. They were not. He did not verify. The system relied on trust. The trust was broken. Nobody in the system detected the failure. The Baliscan government detected the failure. The Okaiken system did not.

The Okami Times notes: two systems failed. Two whistleblowers did not. The Akamatsu Group whistleblower — a mid-level accountant — had the courage to speak. The Baliscan government — a foreign government — had the courage to blockade. The Okaiken system — the SESC, the Ministry of Environment, the routine audits — did not have the courage or the competence to detect ¥18B in missing pension funds or 40,000 tonnes of uninspected waste.

The IAA Commission has teeth. Justice Aoyama has teeth. The first three referrals — Harujima, Kizan, Akamatsu Group — are the right referrals. But the Okami Times asks: how many more Akamatsu Groups are there? How many more Tanaka Hiroshis are there? The system failed twice this week. The whistleblowers did not. The system must be fixed. The whistleblowers must be protected. The workers must be compensated. The waste must be inspected. The system must answer for its failures.

And the Okami Times asks: what is the connection between the Akamatsu Group's ¥48M in political donations to the LDP and the SESC's failure to detect ¥18B in missing pension funds? The donations were legal. The timing was not. The POIA is investigating. The connection must be examined. The workers paid. The executives donated. The regulators did not audit. The connection must be investigated. The system must answer.

'''THE AYORIN COMMUNITY — The Apology Is a Beginning. It Is Not an Ending.'''
The FM apologised. The Ayorin community accepted the apology. The community meets Friday. The community will decide what comes next.

The Okami Times notes: the FM spoke for 12 minutes. The FM answered questions for 28 minutes. The FM apologised in one sentence. The community waited eleven days. The ratio does not balance. The apology is accepted. The delay is not explained. The questions will continue. The Diet will ask. The community will meet. The FM's words will be tested.

The Ayorin community is not a footnote in the Castellano story. The Ayorin community is not a secondary story. The Ayorin attacks are a crisis. The Castellano is a crisis. The Foreign Ministry addressed one crisis and ignored the other for eleven days. The FM apologised. The apology is a beginning. It is not an ending. The community will decide what comes next. The Okami Times will be there.

